<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Xiang of Zhou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 619 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Xiang of Zhou was king of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> from 651 to 619 BCE. Early in his reign he honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a> as hegemon at the covenant of Kuiqiu. Driven from his capital by his half-brother Prince Dai in 636 BCE, he was restored the following year by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin">Duke Wen of Jin</a>, showing the dependence of the Zhou kings on the great feudal states.</p></div>
